  "query_preview": "..\nTonight into Wednesday morning: Conditions remain dry through \nWednesday morning in between weather systems. Areas of fog are \nexpected to redevelop overnight across the Columbia Basin, \nCascade valleys, and valleys across Northeast WA and North ID \nwith chances for visibilities less than two miles ranging from \n30-60 percent across the aforementioned areas.\n\nWednesday afternoon into Thursday: Chances for precipitation\nincrease as a low pressure system moves inland over Oregon and\nsouthern WA. While the bulk of the dynamics will remain south \nof the region, a deformation zone will set up to the northwest\nof the low, resulting in a band of precipitation developing\nacross central and southern WA as moisture wraps around the low\nand gets drawn into the deformation field. In addition to the\ndeformation band, northeast flow into the central Cascades will\nresult in upslope flow and orographically enhanced precipitation\nover Blewett Pass and Mission Ridge. \n\nThose traveling along Highway 97 south of Leavenworth over \nBlewett Pass should expect 3-7 inches of snow. The latest NBM \nprobabilities indicate a 50 percent chance of 4+ inches and a 30\npercent chance of 7+ inches. Probabilities are similar for\nMission Ridge. The deformation band loses its punch further \nnorth, so it's unlikely that there will be enough moisture to\nsee impacts outside of this small portion of the region \nWednesday night into Thursday morning. \n\nThursday afternoon through Friday: Another period of dry\nconditions is forecast for the end of the week with temperatures\ntrending cooler. By Thursday into Friday, temperatures will be\nnear seasonal normals for mid to late November. High \ntemperatures are expected to be in the upper 30s to low 40s \nwith lows in the 20s to low 30s.\n\nSaturday through Monday: Cooler temperatures combined with the\narrival of another upper level low will bring potential for \nwidespread lowland snowfall over the weekend. Snow accumulations \nwill depend on the timing of the moisture and the track of the \nlow. Uncertainty persists as to how close the upper low will \ntrack toward the Northwest. It is typical during El Nino \nwinters for lows to dive further south as we have seen with the\npast couple of lows, but generally models are depicting this\nweekend low tracking further north into WA and OR. If models \ncontinue to show this northerly track, lowland snowfall amounts\nbetween 1 and 3 inches could be expected with this system. Check\nback throughout this week for updates as the weekend \napproaches. /Fewkes",
